meta {
  name: Link person to an existing CAS identity
  type: http
  seq: 15
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/persons/link
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "identifier": "kavya.nair@example.com",
    "first_name": "Kavya",
    "last_name": "Nair",
    "display_name": "Kavya Nair"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 501
}

docs {
  The sibling of request 14, for someone who already has a CAS account: look
  the identity up by username or email, then write the local person row against
  the id that lookup returns. It exists so a parent with two children at two
  schools is one CAS identity and two person rows, rather than two identities.

  501, and unlike request 14 this one is asserted rather than left open. The
  difference is where the answer comes from. FindUser maps every non-2xx from
  CAS to ErrLookupUnsupported, which this service reports as 501, so whatever
  CAS does short of answering the lookup successfully, the status here is
  decided by our code and can be established by reading it. Request 13's status
  is decided by CAS's behaviour, which reading our code cannot tell you.

  Note the deliberate asymmetry with a search that finds nothing: "CAS cannot be
  asked" is 501, and would be wrong to report as 404 "no such user", which is a
  fact we are not in a position to state.

  The one way this returns 201 is CAS answering the lookup with a matching user.
  If that ever happens, this request creates a person row that folder 12 does
  not delete - the same gap request 18 documents.

  Until CAS grows the endpoint, supply cas_user_id to POST /persons directly,
  as request 02 does.
}
